#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168064 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6638787f-3ef3-5623-bcec-de4b89ebfd02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4962 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168065 :tutorial/content "Suppose we fix a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ with scalars from $\\mathbb{R}$ (note that \"finite-dimensional\" means that the basis contains a finite number of elements). Then, we can represent any element $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ using the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c9297a30f87baf08295a752cb23f2d314139508f875b4ffa2fffc8763656bd54.svg)\n\nThis defines a bijective mapping $\\phi: V\\to \\mathbb{R}^n.$ Moreover, this particular mapping has some nice properties:\n\n- $\\phi(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}) = \\phi(\\mathbf{u}) + \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in V,$ and\n\n- $\\phi(\\alpha \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nBecause of this, we say that $V$ is **isomorphic** to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Intuitively, this means that $V$ and $\\mathbb R^n$ have identical structures.\n\nSo, any finite-dimensional vector space is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for some particular $n.$ This means that instead of studying all possible vector spaces, we can simply study only one family of vector spaces, namely $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e501b24f-7ea0-5110-a4c5-e8739ea9ac11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4964 :tutorial/title "Isomorphisms of Vector Spaces"}]}
